export const NORTHVALE_CANONICAL_LORE={"beforeTheTown":"The North Fork valley lies within the ancestral homelands of the Nuche, or Ute peoples. Long before mining claims, ski maps, and real-estate brochures, the valley was part of a much larger living landscape of seasonal movement, hunting, trade, ceremony, and family memory. Modern NorthVale acknowledges that history imperfectly: the town funds cultural consultation and watershed work, while residents still debate whether acknowledgement has translated into meaningful authority.","miningFoundation":"North Vale began in 1884 as a silver camp where Alder Creek meets the North Fork. The richest claims lasted less than twenty years, but a rail spur, Miners Hall, boarding houses, repair shops, and a stubborn year-round population kept the settlement alive after the boom. The town's oldest families are not cattle barons or aristocrats; they are descendants of cooks, laborers, shopkeepers, railway workers, teachers, miners, and migrants who learned how to survive an expensive, isolated valley together.","whiteSunday":"On February 18, 1912, a massive avalanche crossed the old mine road during a church and market day remembered as White Sunday. Forty-one people died and dozens more were rescued by neighbors using shovels, timber, and improvised sleds. The disaster created NorthVale's culture of mountain responsibility: local status means little when weather turns, safety knowledge is shared, and anyone who ignores a closure endangers the people who must come after them.","skiTown":"In 1947, returning mountain soldiers, former miners, and local teachers built a rope tow on the north-facing slope above town. What began as a community winter project became NorthVale Mountain. The resort grew in stages rather than appearing fully formed, and generations of residents worked lifts, patrolled runs, taught lessons, cooked, tuned equipment, cleaned rooms, and opened businesses around it. The mountain is both the town's economic engine and the source of its most persistent arguments.","valeAccord":"The Vale Accord of 1978 allowed a major resort expansion only after the operator committed to public trail access, watershed monitoring, search-and-rescue funding, community passes, and a permanent contribution to worker housing. Its fifty-year renewal window opened in 2026. Almost everyone agrees the Accord matters; almost nobody agrees what a modern version should require or how much the town can demand without jeopardizing the jobs that keep it alive.","modernDay":"NorthVale is a real town beneath the destination image: public schools, a clinic, apartments above old storefronts, service alleys behind polished lodges, exhausted shift workers, competitive athletes, families, artists, and people who arrived for one winter fifteen years ago. Peak season is crowded, intimate, and slightly reckless. Mud season strips the performance away. Summer replaces skis with bikes, horses, climbing, lake days, trail work, weddings, and construction. Autumn is brief, golden, and full of decisions.","presentSeason":"The current winter begins after a warm, dry autumn and an abrupt series of early storms. NorthVale Mountain proposes replacing its aging Sunrise Gondola and opening controlled access into Silver Basin. Juniper House, the largest seasonal-worker residence, may be sold after the season. New snowpack data raises safety questions that are real but not simple. A missing page has also resurfaced from the White Sunday patrol record. These threads overlap, but there is no single villain—only people with competing responsibilities, incomplete information, and private reasons to care."} as const;

